 Sequence-dependent errors with no cross-contamination risk include touching (or removing) items in reverse order from an item that was previously touched. This usually occurs because the person does not remember what happened next during their personal protective equipment (PPE) donning process.
For example: The next-to-last step in your organization's PPE removal procedure involves removing your respirator facepiece before you remove any other piece of PPE. If someone follows this sequence incorrectly by first taking off his/her head covering without also removing his mask, then he has broken protocol even though there is no actual cross-contamination between these two pieces of gear (the hair covering may come into contact with mucous membranes when it touches them). However, if they do get contaminated afterward due either directly through bodily fluids like blood or indirectly via objects such as hand tools used for decontaminating themselves afterwards; this constitutes potential exposure risks both towards others who might subsequently use those same facilities without proper precautions AND toward himself!
What Are The Steps In Ppe Donning And Doffing Order
The following steps should be taken while putting on Personal Protective Equipment (PPE):
Wash hands thoroughly before entering work area;
Put on gloves;
Gown up;
Don appropriate respiratory protection according to local requirements;
Follow standard precautions such as frequent hand washing, cleaning surfaces regularly etc., but never touch your eyes nose mouth until AFTER completing all other tasks related specifically TO caring for patients OR performing maintenance activities within hospital setting."
