It is important to follow the correct procedures for putting on and removing your PPE to prevent contamination. Contamination can occur when you move around in your workspace wearing PPE, especially if you’re not careful about how you handle it. You must always wear clean gloves when working near open wounds or body fluids since bacteria and viruses spread easily through contact with these

 First and foremost, one has to understand what risk management is all about. The objective behind any form of safety/health/risk (SHR) or environmental regulation is to prevent accidents and other negative health outcomes.
One way of doing this is to put in place regulations that force organizations to take care of their workers' wellbeing. In many cases, this means putting in procedures and systems whose implementation requires an active commitment on behalf of organizational managers.
In some cases, however, these measures fail to motivate the desired behavior change, even when there are no problems with incentives, rewards or punishment, nor is it simply a question of worker ignorance. When this happens, we need to ask ourselves whether it is possible to improve things through better communication strategies.
There could be various reasons for such failure:
  1. Lack of employee involvement in decision-making;
  2. Poorly designed policies and/or practices which do not respond appropriately to individual needs;
  3. Insufficient training given to staff members regarding new responsibilities arising out of changes implemented within companies etc.;
If so far everything seems fine then you can conclude that nothing more needs to be done since people already know what they must do but refuse to comply because someone else makes them feel guilty! This leads us back to our original assumption about “managers endorsing” rules and procedures whereas others appear merely as necessary evils imposed upon them without proper consultation. It follows therefore that anything said or written will inevitably lose its impact unless combined with effective action taken against those who violate established norms.
Hence before moving ahead let’s try answering following questions:
Do existing measures really work? Or maybe just create illusions where reality differs substantially from perception? Who decides whether rule was broken anyway – manager himself/herself (i.e., top-down approach) or line-level supervisor (bottom-up)? And finally does enforcement mean automatic termination regardless off seriousness of offense? Only after getting satisfactory answers
